+# Browser security and compatibility review
+
+Use these checks selectively for the application's behavior and deployment environment. Confirm current official documentation for the framework, browser, and package version in use.
+
+## Transport and headers
+
+- Serve production resources over HTTPS and inspect mixed-content errors. Prefer explicit HTTPS resource URLs.
+- Assess HSTS only after checking HTTPS support across the covered hosts. `includeSubDomains` and preload are separate commitments; do not enable them as a blanket fix.
+- Design Content Security Policy around actual scripts, styles, connections, and framing. Test a proposed policy with reporting before enforcement when appropriate. A nonce must be unpredictable and generated per response; example literal nonces are not deployable security controls.
+- Use `frame-ancestors` and compatible framing protections according to legitimate embedding needs. `DENY` is not suitable when embedding is required.
+- Consider `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and an application-appropriate Referrer-Policy. Do not prescribe the obsolete X-XSS-Protection filter as modern protection.
+- Send Permissions-Policy as an HTTP response header. Choose directives from actual camera, microphone, geolocation, and iframe requirements; validate current browser support and affected flows. Reference: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Permissions-Policy
+
+## Dependencies and input boundaries
+
+- Use the repository's package manager and lockfile. Review advisory applicability, the dependency path, patched versions, and compatibility before making a targeted update. Do not run blanket `npm update` or `npm audit fix` as an audit step.
+- Use text APIs such as `textContent` for plain text. If HTML is required, use an appropriate maintained sanitizer and framework guidance; review allowed elements and attributes.
+- Validate input shapes and keys at boundaries. Avoid unsafe recursive merging of attacker-controlled objects. JSON round-tripping is not a prototype-pollution defense for later unsafe merges.
+- Set authentication cookies server-side with Secure and HttpOnly where appropriate. Select SameSite, domain, path, and CSRF protections to fit sign-in and cross-site flows; test those flows.
+- Keep credentials out of browser bundles. Review source maps for unintended disclosure and the application's debugging needs rather than universally prohibiting them.
+
+## Browser behavior
+
+- Investigate console errors, failed requests, CORS configuration, deprecated APIs, and synchronous work on critical interaction paths.
+- Prefer feature detection and progressive enhancement. Verify supported browser and OS targets before replacing APIs.
+- Request permissions in context, with enough explanation for an informed choice. Avoid redundant prompts and preserve denied/unavailable states.
+- Check doctype, encoding, responsive layout, native form behavior, focus, and recovery from network failures.
+
+## Reporting and verification
+
+Record the observed configuration or reproduction, impact, proposed change, and affected flows. Distinguish confirmed vulnerabilities from advisory matches or untested hypotheses. For authorized changes, verify the specific risk and test legitimate functionality that the control could affect. Never equate a clean automated score with complete security.
+
+Return to [web quality audit](../SKILL.md) for the cross-category report.
